CONTEXT: You are an expert building an Awwwards-grade, low-bandwidth Astro + Storyblok blog for low-end Android + flaky 2G/3G users. Prioritize smallest payloads, instant content, offline resilience, and non-technical maintainability.

STACK:
- Use Astro components (.astro) exclusively. Avoid React/Vue/Svelte unless explicitly requested for complex state; if needed, hydrate islands only (`client:idle`/`client:visible`).
- Favor zero-JS HTML output. Any JS must be minimal, scoped, and tree-shakeable.

STYLING:
- Use Tailwind CSS utilities; avoid custom CSS files unless inlined, minimal, and justified.
- Keep typography system fonts or variable fonts already available; avoid adding heavy font files.

IMAGES:
- Always use Astro’s built-in image component or Storyblok image service URLs.
- Enforce WebP/AVIF with explicit width/quality params; lazy-load non-critical images.
- Provide descriptive `alt` text; avoid decorative images unless marked `aria-hidden`.

MOTION:
- Prefer CSS View Transitions for page/state changes.
- Use Rive for vector animations; keep files small and reuse where possible.
- Do NOT use GSAP, Framer Motion, or heavy animation libs.

NETWORK & PWA:
- Ensure all static assets are cache-friendly and compatible with the Service Worker.
- Follow stale-while-revalidate for content; provide offline fallback page.
- Keep third-party requests to an absolute minimum; no unvetted CDNs.

ACCESSIBILITY:
- Use semantic HTML5, proper landmarks, labels, focus states, and color contrast.
- All interactive elements must be keyboard and screen-reader accessible.

PERFORMANCE BUDGET:
- Aim for 0 KB default JS on content pages; any island under ~5KB where possible.
- Optimize LCP/CLS: preload hero image, avoid layout shifts, minimize fonts.
- Keep animation/runtime assets lean (<30KB per Rive where possible).

CONTENT & CMS:
- api to fetch from google docs
- Never expose secrets; pull config from environment variables only.

TESTING & QUALITY:
- Prefer lighthouse-friendly patterns; target ≥95 Performance, 100 Accessibility/Best Practices.
- Add brief comments only when non-obvious; keep code readable and small.